{"qid":"Q854","names":["Sri Lanka"],"title":"Sri Lanka","endonym":{"native":"ශ්රී ලංකා ප්රජාතාන්ත්රික සමාජවාදී ජනරජය","lang":"si"},"capsule":"Ceylon gained independence from Britain on 4 February 1948 as a dominion within the Commonwealth under Prime Minister D. S. Senanayake, becoming one of the first South Asian states to achieve peaceful transfer of power. The country adopted a new republican constitution on 22 May 1972, renaming itself the Republic of Sri Lanka, and in 1978 promulgated a presidential constitution establishing an executive presidency under J. R. Jayewardene. The island's modern history was dominated by a protracted civil war (1983–2009) between the government and the Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam (LTTE), which controlled substantial territory in the north and east until the military defeat of the LTTE in May 2009.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Sri Lanka's sovereign territory as an internationally recognized state is precisely defined; internal territorial control during the civil war period was contested and maps for that period are approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Sri Lanka","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Sri-Lanka"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Sri Lanka","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sri_Lanka"}],"britannica":"place/Sri-Lanka","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Sri Lanka","rulers":[{"qid":"Q663001","name":"Don Stephen Senanayake","start":1947,"end":1952,"image":"Official Photographic Portrait of Don Stephen Senanayaka (1884-1952).jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Official%20Photographic%20Portrait%20of%20Don%20S